\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Passage Commentary\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Mark 9:30-32 | Jesus Again Teaches The Cross And Resurrection\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 Jesus and his disciples leave that place and pass through Galilee, but Jesus does not want anyone to know where they are because he is teaching his disciples (vv. 30-31). The privacy matters. Jesus is not hiding because he lacks courage. He is withdrawing so the disciples can be formed. They must learn the meaning of his mission before they can understand their own.\
His teaching is direct: "The Son of Man is going to be delivered into the hands of men. They will kill him, and after three days he will rise" (v. 31, NIV). The language of being "delivered" is heavy. Jesus will not merely be misunderstood or inconvenienced. He will be handed over to human violence. Yet he also says he will rise. Death is real, but it will not be final.\
The disciples do not understand what he means, and they are afraid to ask (v. 32). That fear is spiritually dangerous. When confusion stays hidden, it often grows into distorted behavior. Instead of bringing their questions to Jesus, the disciples carry their misunderstanding down the road. Soon it appears as ambition.\
This is already a word of mercy for us. Jesus' disciples can be near him and still not understand him. They can hear true words and still resist their meaning. But fear keeps them from asking, and silence keeps them from receiving correction. Discipleship requires bringing our confusion into the light before Jesus.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 Mark 9:33-37 | Jesus Redefines Greatness Around Servanthood And Welcome\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 When they arrive in Capernaum, Jesus asks, "What were you arguing about on the road?" (v. 33, NIV). They keep quiet because they had argued about who was the greatest (v. 34). Their silence here is shame. They know the argument does not fit the Teacher they are following.\
Jesus sits down, calls the Twelve, and says, "Anyone who wants to be first must be the very last, and the servant of all" (v. 35, NIV). He does not say greatness is bad. He redefines it. In the kingdom, greatness is not rank over others but service beneath others. The first place is found by taking the last place. Authority is reshaped by the cross.\
But Jesus does not leave the teaching as an idea. He takes a child and places the child among them. Then he takes the child in his arms (v. 36). In a room full of disciples concerned about rank, Jesus centers someone with no rank to offer them. The child cannot advance their status. The child cannot improve their reputation. The child can only be received.\
Then Jesus says, "Whoever welcomes one of these little children in my name welcomes me" (v. 37, NIV). This is a stunning claim. Jesus so identifies with the low-status and vulnerable that receiving them in his name is receiving him. And whoever receives him receives not only Jesus but the One who sent him (v. 37). The disciples are arguing about proximity to greatness, and Jesus tells them greatness is standing in front of them in the person they are tempted to overlook.\
This corrects more than ambition. It corrects the way spiritual communities measure value. A church may say beautiful things about Jesus while still organizing itself around impressive people, useful people, wealthy people, gifted people, or socially connected people. Jesus places a child in the middle and asks whether we can recognize him there.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 Mark 9:38-41 | Jesus Corrects Possessive Ministry\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 John then says, "Teacher, we saw someone driving out demons in your name and we told him to stop, because he was not one of us" (v. 38, NIV). The phrase "not one of us" reveals the problem. John is concerned about group boundaries and insider control. Someone is acting in Jesus' name, but because he does not belong to their circle, the disciples try to stop him.\
Jesus corrects them: "Do not stop him" (v. 39, NIV). He does not say names and truth do not matter. The man is acting in Jesus' name. But Jesus refuses the disciples' possessive instinct. The kingdom is not their private franchise. Ministry in Jesus' name is not owned by the Twelve.\
"Whoever is not against us is for us" (v. 40, NIV). In this moment, Jesus widens their imagination. Their instinct is to protect status by controlling who counts. Jesus teaches them to rejoice in faithful work done in his name, even when it does not increase their importance.\
He even says that anyone who gives them a cup of water because they belong to the Messiah will not lose their reward (v. 41). The kingdom notices small acts done in relation to Jesus. A cup of water matters. Hidden service matters. The disciples are arguing about greatness, but Jesus sees the smallest mercy offered in his name.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 Mark 9:42-48 | Jesus Warns Against Harming Little Ones And Tolerating Sin\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 Jesus' tone becomes severe: "If anyone causes one of these little ones--those who believe in me--to stumble, it would be better for them if a large millstone were hung around their neck and they were thrown into the sea" (v. 42, NIV). We should not soften this too quickly. Jesus is fiercely protective of little ones who believe in him.\
The "little ones" may include children, vulnerable disciples, and those whose faith is easily harmed by the powerful, proud, careless, or self-protective. Jesus has just placed a child in the center. Now he warns anyone who would cause such a one to stumble. This means holiness is not only about my private spiritual condition. My sin can damage others. My ambition, pride, harshness, hypocrisy, abuse of power, or careless example can wound the vulnerable.\
Then Jesus uses shocking images about hand, foot, and eye (vv. 43-48). He is not commanding literal self-mutilation. He is teaching that sin must be resisted with radical seriousness. If something leads us into sin, we cannot make peace with it. We cannot treat it as harmless. We cannot protect it because it feels useful, familiar, pleasurable, or tied to our identity.\
The imagery is severe because the stakes are severe. Jesus speaks of hell, where "the worms that eat them do not die, and the fire is not quenched" (v. 48, NIV). He is warning against the destruction that comes when sin is cherished rather than cut off. The same Jesus who takes a child in his arms also warns fiercely against sin that harms the soul and harms the little ones.\
This passage must be handled carefully. Jesus' words should not be used to create panic, self-hatred, or scrupulous fear. He is not inviting people to despise their bodies. He is calling disciples to stop negotiating with what destroys. Grace does not make sin light. Grace makes it possible to bring sin into the light and turn from it with urgency.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 Mark 9:49-50 | Jesus Calls His People To Salt And Peace\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 Jesus ends with salt: "Everyone will be salted with fire" (v. 49, NIV). The image is difficult, but it gathers the passage's themes of purification, sacrifice, judgment, and distinctiveness. Following Jesus is not a bland life. His people are purified, tested, and made distinct.\
"Salt is good, but if it loses its saltiness, how can you make it salty again?" (v. 50, NIV). Salt that is no longer salty has lost the very quality that makes it useful. Disciples who abandon Jesus' way of servanthood, welcome, holiness, and peace lose the distinctiveness of kingdom life.\
Then Jesus says, "Have salt among yourselves, and be at peace with each other" (v. 50, NIV). The final word is not competition but peace. But this peace is not shallow politeness. It is the peace of a community no longer fighting for greatness, no longer excluding out of insecurity, no longer harming little ones, no longer making peace with sin.\
Jesus is forming a people who taste different from the world because they live by a different order. The world argues about greatness. Jesus takes the last place. The world overlooks the vulnerable. Jesus receives the child. The world protects status. Jesus honors hidden cups of water. The world excuses sin when it serves desire. Jesus calls for costly holiness. The world calls avoidance peace. Jesus creates peace through truth, humility, protection, and love.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Mark 9:30-50 In One Movement\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 Mark 9:30-50 shows how slowly the cross reshapes the disciples. Jesus teaches again that he will be delivered, killed, and raised, but the disciples do not understand and are afraid to ask. Their hidden confusion becomes open ambition as they argue about greatness. Jesus answers by becoming a teacher of reversal: the first must be last, the great must become servant, and the child must be received as receiving Jesus himself. He corrects their possessive instinct toward ministry, fiercely warns against causing little ones to stumble, calls for radical resistance to sin, and ends by calling them to salt and peace. The passage is not a collection of disconnected sayings. It is Jesus forming a cross-shaped community: humble, welcoming, generous, holy, protective, distinct, and at peace.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Scripture Cross-References\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Isaiah 53:6-12 | The servant is handed over, suffers, and bears the sins of many, deepening Jesus' passion prediction.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Mark 10:13-16 | Jesus again receives children, reinforcing their place in the kingdom and correcting the disciples' instincts.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Matthew 18:1-10 | A parallel teaching on children, humility, stumbling, and the Father's concern for little ones.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Philippians 2:3-8 | Christ's humility and servant form ground the call to reject selfish ambition.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}1 Corinthians 12:21-26 | The body must honor weaker and less visible members, echoing Jesus' welcome of the low-status.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Colossians 4:6 | Speech seasoned with salt helps frame distinctiveness as grace-filled, wise witness.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 The Shape of a Kingdom People\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 A kingdom people let the cross dethrone status. Jesus is on the road toward being delivered up, killed, and raised, yet the disciples are arguing about rank. This is not just their problem. We also know how to turn faith into comparison, ministry into self-importance, and closeness to Jesus into a way to feel superior. Jesus breaks that logic by calling the first to become last and servant of all.\
A kingdom people receive and protect the vulnerable. Jesus places a child in the center because discipleship is tested by how we treat those who cannot repay us, advance us, or defend themselves. That means churches, homes, and friendships cannot measure health only by giftedness, energy, growth, or influence. Jesus asks whether the little ones are welcomed, honored, and protected.\
A kingdom people resist sin for the sake of love. Jesus' severe words about hand, foot, eye, and hell are not detached from the child in his arms. Sin is never merely private when it damages others, forms a community, or causes the vulnerable to stumble. Grace does not teach us to tolerate destruction. It teaches us to cut off what destroys so that peace can become real.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Gospel Shaped Reflection\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 From this passage we learn that Jesus' death and resurrection create a new kind of community. He gives himself into the lowest place, and then he forms disciples who no longer need to fight for the highest place. We begin with what Jesus has done and who he is, because only the cross can free us from ambition, possessiveness, careless harm, and false peace.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 Who is God?\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}God saves through the self-giving Son who is delivered, killed, and raised (v. 31).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}God values the low-status and vulnerable so deeply that receiving them in Jesus' name is receiving him (v. 37).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}God notices small acts of mercy done in relation to Jesus, even a cup of water (v. 41).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}God is fiercely protective of little ones who believe in Jesus (v. 42).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}God is holy and loving; he will not treat destructive sin as harmless.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}God forms his people to be distinct, salty, and at peace (v. 50).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 What has God done?\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}God has sent the Son of Man to be delivered, killed, and raised after three days (v. 31).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Jesus has exposed the disciples' ambition and redefined greatness as becoming last and servant of all (v. 35).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Jesus has placed a child at the center and identified himself with the vulnerable received in his name (vv. 36-37).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Jesus has corrected possessive ministry and honored even small acts done because someone belongs to him (vv. 39-41).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Jesus has warned his people not to cause little ones to stumble and not to make peace with sin (vv. 42-48).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 Who are we in light of what God has done?\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We are not people who need to secure worth by being greatest.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We are disciples of the crucified and risen Son, called to take the last place in love.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We are servants who meet Jesus in the welcome of the vulnerable.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We are not owners of Jesus' mission; we are participants in work done in his name.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We are guardians of little ones, responsible for how our lives affect vulnerable believers.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We are a salty people, made distinct for holiness and peace.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 What wrong behavior, harmful emotions, or false attitudes result when we forget these truths?\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We argue about greatness while Jesus is teaching us the way of the cross.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We hide confusion and fear instead of bringing our questions to Jesus.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We measure people by usefulness, visibility, giftedness, money, age, or status.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We become possessive of ministry and suspicious of faithful work that does not belong to our circle.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We minimize sin when it benefits us or protects our reputation.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We cause little ones to stumble through pride, harshness, hypocrisy, abuse of power, or careless example.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We call avoidance "peace" while refusing the humility and holiness that make real peace possible.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 How would we live if we believed these truths?\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We would ask Jesus honest questions instead of hiding misunderstanding behind silence.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We would pursue the last place without using servanthood as a new way to be admired.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We would welcome children, vulnerable believers, overlooked people, and low-status neighbors as receiving Jesus.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We would rejoice when others serve faithfully in Jesus' name, even when it does not increase our importance.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We would take sin seriously because love takes harm seriously.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We would cut off patterns, practices, and permissions that lead us or others toward destruction.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We would pursue peace that is truthful, protective, humble, and holy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 If we lived this out, how would it impact our neighborhood, family, church, workplace, and friends?\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Our homes would become less competitive and more attentive to the youngest, weakest, quietest, and most easily overlooked.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Our churches would become safer for little ones because status would no longer protect harmful behavior.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Our friendships would become places where honest questions are welcomed instead of hidden in fear.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Our workplaces would see people who serve without turning service into self-promotion.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Our neighborhoods would encounter a community that honors small acts of mercy as meaningful in Jesus' name.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Our public witness would become more credible because our holiness would protect people, not merely preserve appearances.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Our shared life would taste different: less rivalry, less possessiveness, less harm, more welcome, more courage, more peace.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Hearing the Father's Voice\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 The Bible is God's love letter to us. When we read it, we do not want accusation, fear, shame, or distance to distort what God has spoken. In this passage, the Father shows us his Son walking toward the lowest place and then forming us into people who no longer have to fight for the highest place. The words that follow are written to help us hear the Father's love through the passage we have just studied.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb80\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 My child, you do not have to become great to be held by me. My Son went down into the lowest place for you, and in him I free you from the exhausting need to rise above others. Do not be afraid to become small in love. I meet you there.\
Receive the ones the world overlooks. Guard the little ones. Let go of the sin that harms you and wounds others. I am not calling you into a cold or anxious holiness, but into a life that tastes like my kingdom: humble, clean, courageous, merciful, and at peace. In my Son, the last place is not abandonment. It is the place where love becomes visible.\
}